```typescript
// Define the necessary access levels for the sub-agent
interface AccessLevel {
  READ: string;
  WRITE: string;
  EXECUTE: string;
  DELETE: string;
}

// Define the permissions configuration
interface PermissionsConfig {
  subAgent: {
    accessLevels: AccessLevel;
    resources: string[];
  };
}

// Update the permissions to include all necessary access levels
const permissionsConfig: PermissionsConfig = {
  subAgent: {
    accessLevels: {
      READ: 'read',
      WRITE: 'write',
      EXECUTE: 'execute',
      DELETE: 'delete',
    },
    resources: [
      '/resource1',
      '/resource2',
      '/resource3',
      // Add any additional resources as needed
    ],
  },
};

// Export the updated permissions configuration
export default permissionsConfig;
```

Note: The above code is a sample representation and might need to be adjusted according to the actual requirements and existing codebase of your project. The `resources` array should be populated with the actual resources that the sub-agent needs to access. 

If you have an existing `permissions.config` file, you would need to merge the above changes into your existing file, ensuring that the necessary access levels and resources are included. 

Also, ensure that the file is in the correct format (e.g., JSON, TypeScript, etc.) as per your project's requirements. The above code is in TypeScript format. If your project uses a different format, you would need to adjust the code accordingly.